PAGES = [
guide("google-business-profile-suspended", "google business profile suspended", "Google Business Profile suspended", "Google Business Profile",
      "Google Business Profile Suspended? How to Fix It",
      "Google Business Profile suspended? How Anaheim and Orange County businesses can find the cause, gather evidence and appeal with Google's appeals tool.",
      "Google Business Profile Suspended: How to Find the Cause and Appeal",
      "If your Google Business Profile is suspended, Google has decided it does not follow its guidelines. Fix the problem first, gather evidence such as a business license, registration or utility bill that matches your profile's name and address, then appeal once through Google's appeals tool. Do not create a new profile. Anaheim and Orange County businesses can usually trace a suspension to the address, name or category.",
      ["Suspensions come from guideline problems.", "Fix the cause before you appeal.", "Evidence must match your name and address.",
       "You can appeal each decision only once.", "Never create a replacement profile."],
      [("What a suspension means", "<p>Google's help page says it may suspend or disable Business Profiles that do not follow its guidelines. A suspended profile can disappear from Maps and Search or lose the ability to be managed, which for a local business in Anaheim or anywhere in Orange County means fewer calls overnight. The good news is that many suspensions have a clear cause, and Google provides an appeals tool that tells you which decision was made, when, and which policy it relates to.</p>"),
       ("Common causes to check first", "<p>Most suspensions trace back to Google's guidelines for representing your business. Check these before anything else:</p><ul><li>An address you do not operate from. Google says virtual offices are not eligible and P.O. boxes are not acceptable.</li><li>A business name with added keywords or city names that do not appear on your signage and paperwork.</li><li>A storefront address for a business that actually visits customers, which should be set up as a service-area business.</li><li>Categories for services you do not offer.</li><li>Several profiles for the same business at the same location.</li></ul>"),
       ("Step 1: fix the problem", "<p>Before you appeal, make the profile follow the guidelines. Remove keywords from the name so it matches your real-world name, correct the address or switch to a service area if you serve customers at their locations, and remove categories that do not fit. Google's help page is explicit that you should make sure your profile follows all the guidelines before you submit an appeal, because the appeal is reviewed against the profile as it stands.</p>"),
       ("Step 2: gather evidence", "<p>The appeals tool lets you attach optional evidence, and Google lists the kinds that can strengthen an appeal: official business registration, a business license, tax certificates and utility bills for the business such as electricity, phone, water or internet. Google's tip is to check that the business name and address on every document match the profile you are appealing. A mismatch, such as a utility bill in a different name, weakens the case.</p>"),
       ("Step 3: submit one appeal", "<p>Open the Google Business Profile appeals tool while signed in to the Google Account that manages the profile, select the profile and the decision, then attach your files and explain in the text box why the profile should be reinstated. Google notes that appeals can only be submitted once, so wait until everything is fixed and attached. Google reviews the appeal and sends the decision by email.</p>"),
       ("Do not create a new profile", "<p>Google's help page says plainly: do not create a new Business Profile for the same business. A replacement profile does not solve the underlying problem, can look like an attempt to get around the decision and can make things worse. Work through the appeal for the existing profile instead.</p>"),
       ("Track the status", "<p>The appeals tool shows the status of each appeal: Submitted, Approved, Not Approved, Can't be appealed or Eligible for appeal. Check it rather than resubmitting. If your request is denied, Google says it may be able to do an additional review, and you can provide additional evidence that was not included with the original appeal.</p>"),
       ("When the whole account is restricted", "<p>Google explains that an account may be restricted after a policy violation. In that case, the Business Profiles the account manages are suspended and you cannot create or claim other profiles. You first appeal the account restriction from your My accounts page, then, once it is lifted, appeal the Business Profile suspension.</p>"),
       ("Businesses with many locations", "<p>For more than 10 suspended profiles, the appeals tool asks whether you are requesting bulk reinstatement and lets you attach a spreadsheet with each Business Profile ID and supporting documents. Google stresses that the business name and address on each document should exactly match your registration. Multi-location brands across Orange County should keep these records organized before they are ever needed.</p>"),
       ("How to avoid another suspension", "<p>Keep the name exactly as it appears on your signage, use a real address you operate from or a service area, choose categories that describe what you do and avoid edits you cannot support with documents. When you move, change your name or change ownership, update the profile carefully and keep evidence on file.</p>"),
       ("What it means for AI search", "<p>AI assistants and Google's AI features draw on business listings to answer local questions. A suspended or inconsistent profile removes one of the strongest signals about your business. Restoring an accurate profile, with the same details as your website and other listings, helps search engines and assistants describe you correctly again.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Find the guideline problem, fix it, gather documents that match your name and address, and appeal once through Google's appeals tool. Never create a replacement profile. Chimera, based in Anaheim, helps Orange County businesses clean up profiles and listings so they stay eligible.</p>")],
      [("Why was my Google Business Profile suspended?", "Google suspends profiles that do not follow its guidelines, often because of the address, the business name or the categories."),
       ("How do I get my Google Business Profile reinstated?", "Fix the cause, gather matching evidence such as a business license or utility bill and submit an appeal through Google's appeals tool."),
       ("How many times can I appeal?", "Google says appeals can only be submitted once, though a denied request may be eligible for an additional review."),
       ("Should I create a new profile?", "No. Google says not to create a new Business Profile for the same business."),
       ("What evidence helps an appeal?", "Google lists business registration, a business license, tax certificates and utility bills, with the name and address matching the profile."),
       ("Can a virtual office cause a suspension?", "Yes. Google's guidelines say virtual offices are not eligible.")],
      [GBP, VERIFY, AREA, NAP], ["g_suspend", "g_gbp", "g_rank"],
      [V.flow(4, "How a Google Business Profile appeal works", "The steps in Google's appeals tool, in order.",
              [("Fix", "Make the profile follow Google's guidelines."), ("Gather", "License, registration, tax or utility documents that match."),
               ("Appeal", "Submit once through the appeals tool."), ("Decision", "Google emails the result; check status in the tool.")])],
      ["suspended Google Business Profile", "Google Business Profile appeal", "reinstate Google Business Profile", "Google Maps listing suspended"],
      "My Google Business Profile got suspended. How do I get it back?", "Google Business Profile suspended"),

guide("google-business-profile-verification", "google business profile verification", "Google Business Profile verification", "Google Business Profile",
      "Google Business Profile Verification: Every Method",
      "Google Business Profile verification explained for Anaheim and Orange County businesses: phone, email, video, postcard, timelines and what to avoid.",
      "Google Business Profile Verification: Methods, Timelines and Mistakes to Avoid",
      "Google Business Profile verification proves you own the business so you can manage its details on Google. Google chooses which methods you can use, such as phone or text, email, a video recording, a live video call or a postcard, based on your business type, public information, region and hours. Anaheim and Orange County businesses should prepare proof of location and avoid editing the profile while verifying.",
      ["Verification gives you control of your profile.", "Google decides which methods you can use.",
       "Video verification is recommended when offered.", "Postcard codes expire after 30 days.", "Never share your verification code."],
      [("Why verification matters", "<p>Google's help page explains that you need to verify your business to edit its information, such as name or hours, and to interact with customers. Verification is how you gain ownership of your Business Profile and make sure your information is accurate. Until it is verified, much of what you add, including photos, does not show on Google. For a new business in Anaheim or a company opening a second Orange County location, it is the first step in local search.</p>"),
       ("Before you start", "<p>You need a Google Account, which can use a Gmail address or your own business email. Search for your business name and city to see whether a profile already exists; if it does, claim it instead of creating a duplicate. Make sure the name, address or service area, phone number and category are correct before you request verification, because changes during the process can slow it down.</p>"),
       ("Google chooses the method", "<p>Google says verification methods are determined automatically and cannot be changed. The options available depend on your business type, public information, region and business hours, and some businesses need more than one method. If you are offered video verification, Google recommends using it.</p>"),
       ("Phone, text or email", "<p>If phone or text is offered, select it and enter the code you receive. Google notes that automated phone menus, known as IVR systems, will not get the code, so make sure a person or a phone that receives texts can take it. Email verification works the same way: choose it and follow the steps in the email sent to the address shown.</p>"),
       ("Video recording", "<p>Many businesses verify by recording a short video. Plan it before you press record: show the location, the business and proof that you manage it. Keep it continuous and clear. This method is often faster than waiting for mail, which is why Google recommends it where eligible.</p>"),
       ("Live video call", "<p>Some businesses can verify on a live video call with a Google support representative, available only during the business hours of your location. Google suggests being ready to show outside signs, nearby businesses or a street sign; your products, branded equipment or tools; and proof that you manage the business, such as access to employee-only areas, a cash register or keys that open the premises.</p>"),
       ("Postcard", "<p>If mail is offered, Google sends a postcard with a code to your business address. Google says most codes arrive within 14 days and codes expire after 30 days. While you wait, do not edit your business name, address or category, because that makes the code stop working, and do not request a new code, because that invalidates the one already in the mail.</p>"),
       ("Keep the code private", "<p>Google states that it will never ask for your verification code and that you should not share it with anyone, even people who help manage your profile. Treat unsolicited calls offering to verify or rank your listing with caution, and enter the code yourself on your own profile.</p>"),
       ("Service-area businesses", "<p>Plumbers, cleaners and other businesses that visit customers can still verify. Set the profile up as a service-area business with your central location hidden from customers and a service area for the cities you serve. Video methods often ask you to show tools, vehicles or branded equipment instead of a storefront.</p>"),
       ("Re-verification after edits", "<p>Google's category help notes that adding or editing a category can trigger a request to verify again. Major changes to name or address can do the same. Plan important edits for a time when you can respond quickly.</p>"),
       ("If verification fails", "<p>Check that your details are accurate and match your real signage, documents and website, then try the other methods offered. Google's help suggests contacting support if you still cannot verify.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Claim the existing profile if there is one, get your details right, use video if offered, and do not edit or request new codes while you wait. Chimera, based in Anaheim, helps Orange County businesses set up and verify profiles correctly.</p>")],
      [("How do I verify my Google Business Profile?", "Choose one of the methods Google offers you, such as phone, text, email, video or postcard, and complete the steps."),
       ("Can I choose my verification method?", "No. Google says methods are determined automatically based on business type, public information, region and hours."),
       ("How long does postcard verification take?", "Google says most codes arrive within 14 days, and codes expire after 30 days."),
       ("Why didn't I get the phone code?", "Automated phone systems will not get the code; a person or a phone that receives texts must take it."),
       ("Can I edit my profile while waiting for a postcard?", "No. Editing the name, address or category makes the code stop working."),
       ("Will Google ask for my verification code?", "No. Google says it will never ask for your code, so do not share it.")],
      [GBP, SUSPENDED, AREA, CATS], ["g_verify", "g_cat", "g_gbp"],
      [V.flow(3, "Verification methods Google may offer", "Google decides which of these you can use.",
              [("Phone or text", "Enter the code you receive."), ("Email", "Follow the steps in the email."),
               ("Video", "Record or join a live call showing location and ownership."), ("Postcard", "Most arrive within 14 days; codes expire after 30.")])],
      ["verify Google Business Profile", "Google My Business verification", "Google Business Profile video verification", "Google postcard verification"],
      "How do I verify my new business on Google Maps?", "Google Business Profile verification"),

guide("google-business-profile-categories", "google business profile categories", "Google Business Profile categories", "Google Business Profile",
      "Google Business Profile Categories: How to Choose",
      "How to choose Google Business Profile categories for Anaheim and Orange County businesses: primary versus additional categories, examples and mistakes.",
      "Google Business Profile Categories: How to Choose the Right Primary and Additional Categories",
      "Google Business Profile categories tell Google what your business is, and Google says they affect your local ranking. Choose the most specific primary category that describes your main business, add a few additional categories only for real departments or services, and never add categories for every product. For Anaheim and Orange County businesses, the right category decides which local searches you can appear in.",
      ["Categories affect local ranking.", "Pick the most specific primary category.", "Add a few additional categories, not dozens.",
       "Separately owned departments need their own profile.", "Category changes can trigger re-verification."],
      [("Why categories matter", "<p>Google's help page says your business categories inform customers about what you do and help connect you with people searching for your products or services. It also states directly that the categories you select affect your local ranking on Google. Its example: if your primary category is Pizza restaurant, Google might show you for searches such as Restaurants, Italian restaurants or Pizza. For a business in Anaheim or anywhere in Orange County, the category is one of the strongest relevance signals you control.</p>"),
       ("Choose a specific primary category", "<p>Google advises selecting a primary category that best describes your business and choosing a specific category from the list. Its example is to choose Nail salon instead of Salon. If the specific category you want is not available, choose a general one that describes your business. You cannot create your own category.</p>"),
       ("Use additional categories sparingly", "<p>Additional categories describe special departments or services. Google says not to select a category for every product or service, but to use a few categories from the list. Its example is a grocery store with a bakery and deli, which would choose Grocery store as the primary category and add Bakery and Deli as additional categories.</p>"),
       ("Separate businesses need separate profiles", "<p>If a location contains independent, public-facing departments that are separately owned and operated, Google says they should have their own Business Profiles, and you should not add their categories to yours. Its example is a health club with an independently operated cafe: the cafe gets its own profile with Cafe as the primary category.</p>"),
       ("How to research the right category", "<p>Search for your main service in your city and look at which categories the top local results use; Google shows the primary category under the business name. Compare those with what you actually do. Choose the most specific one that is true for your main business, and use services and your website to describe the rest.</p>"),
       ("Category-specific features", "<p>Google notes that some categories unlock features. Hotels can show class ratings and amenities. Restaurants can add links for orders, reservations and menus, and add menu items. Health and beauty businesses can add a booking button and a list of services. Choosing the right category can therefore change what customers can do from your profile.</p>"),
       ("Categories, services and your website", "<p>Categories set the broad relevance; services and your website add detail. List each service on your profile, and give each main service its own page on your site with the same naming. That consistency helps Google and AI assistants connect searches to your business.</p>"),
       ("Changing categories safely", "<p>Google's help page warns that adding or editing a category may prompt Google to ask you to verify your business again. Make category changes deliberately, one at a time, and watch your performance reports afterwards to see the effect on searches and calls.</p>"),
       ("Common mistakes", "<ul><li>Choosing a broad category when a specific one exists.</li><li>Adding categories for services you do not offer.</li><li>Adding a category for every product.</li><li>Adding a co-located business's category to your profile.</li><li>Changing categories often, which can trigger re-verification.</li></ul>"),
       ("Categories and AI search", "<p>AI assistants that answer local questions rely on structured listings to know what a business does. An accurate, specific category, matched by your website's services and structured data, makes it easier for them to place you in the right answers.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Pick the most specific primary category that is true, add only a few genuine additional categories, and back them up with services and service pages. Chimera, based in Anaheim, reviews categories as part of every Orange County profile audit.</p>")],
      [("Do Google Business Profile categories affect ranking?", "Yes. Google says the categories you select affect your local ranking."),
       ("How many categories should I choose?", "One primary category and a few additional ones for real departments or services; Google says not to add one for every product."),
       ("Can I create my own category?", "No. You must choose from Google's list."),
       ("What if my exact category doesn't exist?", "Choose a general category that describes your business."),
       ("Will changing my category affect verification?", "It might; Google says editing categories can prompt re-verification."),
       ("Should a cafe inside my gym be a category on my profile?", "Not if it is separately owned and operated; it should have its own profile.")],
      [GBP, VERIFY, L("local SEO keywords", "local-seo-keywords"), LOCALSEO], ["g_cat", "g_rank", "g_gbp"],
      [local_rank_flow(1)],
      ["Google business categories", "GBP primary category", "Google Maps categories", "best category for Google Business Profile"],
      "Which Google Business Profile category should I pick for my business?", "Google Business Profile categories"),

guide("google-business-profile-service-area", "google business profile service area", "Google Business Profile service area", "Google Business Profile",
      "Google Business Profile Service Area: Setup Guide",
      "How to set a Google Business Profile service area in Anaheim and Orange County: service-area versus hybrid businesses, the 20-area limit and Google's rules.",
      "Google Business Profile Service Area: How to Set It Up the Right Way",
      "A Google Business Profile service area shows where you serve customers when they do not come to you. Service-area businesses hide their address and list up to 20 cities, postal codes or areas; hybrid businesses show their storefront and add a service area. Google says areas cannot be a radius and should stay within about 2 hours of driving from your base, which suits most Anaheim and Orange County trades.",
      ["Service-area businesses hide their address.", "Hybrid businesses show a storefront and a service area.",
       "You can list up to 20 service areas.", "Radius service areas are no longer supported.", "Keep it within about 2 hours of driving."],
      [("What a service area is", "<p>Google's help page says your service area shows customers where you provide your products and services and helps people find your Business Profile. It is the setting that lets a plumber in Anaheim, a cleaner in Irvine or a mobile mechanic anywhere in Orange County appear for customers in the cities they cover, without showing a home address.</p>"),
       ("Service-area or hybrid business?", "<p>Google defines a service-area business as one that visits or delivers to customers but does not serve them at its business address, with cleaning services and plumbers as examples. A hybrid business serves customers at its address and also visits or delivers to them, such as a dine-in restaurant that delivers. Hybrid businesses can show their storefront address, set hours for when staff are available and add a service area.</p>"),
       ("Remove your address if customers don't visit", "<p>Google is direct: if you do not serve customers at your business address, remove the address from your profile. It also says a business without permanent on-site signage is not eligible as a storefront and should be listed as a service-area business. Service-area businesses can only have one profile for the whole area they serve.</p>"),
       ("Who must have a storefront", "<p>Google notes that businesses with products or services that have an age requirement, such as alcohol, cannabis or weapons, need to have a storefront. They cannot be set up as service-area-only profiles.</p>"),
       ("How to add or edit a service area", "<p>Go to your Business Profile, select Edit profile, then Location, and next to Service area select Edit. Choose a suggested area or type one in, then save. Google says you can set up to 20 service areas based on cities, postal codes or other areas, and that approved edits may take up to 48 hours to show.</p>"),
       ("No more radius areas", "<p>Google's help page says you cannot set a service area as a radius around your business. If you set one up that way in the past, you cannot edit it; you must specify your area by city, postal code or another type of area instead. Many older Orange County profiles still have a radius and need updating.</p>"),
       ("How far is too far", "<p>Google advises that the boundaries of your overall area should not be more than about 2 hours of driving time from where your business is based, and to be as specific and accurate as possible. List the places you genuinely serve, not every city in Southern California.</p>"),
       ("Choosing your 20 areas in Orange County", "<p>Orange County had 3,186,989 residents in the 2020 census, spread across many cities. With 20 slots, start with the cities where your customers actually are and where you can respond quickly, such as Anaheim, Fullerton, Orange, Garden Grove and Santa Ana for a north-county business, then add postal codes or nearby cities that bring real jobs.</p>"),
       ("Service areas and rankings", "<p>Google says local results are mainly based on relevance, distance and popularity. A service area tells Google where you work, but distance from your verified location still plays a part, so a business based in Anaheim will not rank equally everywhere it lists. Back the areas up with real projects and reviews from those cities.</p>"),
       ("Service area pages on your website", "<p>Add a service area page that lists the cities you cover and links to project examples from each. Avoid dozens of near-identical city pages, which Google's spam policies treat as doorway abuse.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Pick the right business type, hide your address if customers do not visit, list up to 20 real areas within about 2 hours of driving and replace any old radius. Chimera, based in Anaheim, sets up service areas for trades across Orange County.</p>")],
      [("How many service areas can I add on Google?", "Up to 20, based on cities, postal codes or other areas."),
       ("Can I set my service area as a radius?", "No. Google no longer supports radius service areas."),
       ("Should I hide my address on Google Business Profile?", "Yes, if you do not serve customers at your business address."),
       ("How far can my service area reach?", "Google says the overall area should not be more than about 2 hours of driving from your base."),
       ("How long do service area changes take?", "Google says approved edits may take up to 48 hours to show."),
       ("Can a service-area business have several profiles?", "No. Google says service-area businesses can only have one profile for the whole area they serve.")],
      [SAB, GBP, L("local landing pages", "local-landing-pages"), VERIFY], ["g_sab", "g_gbp", "g_rank", "spam", "w_oc"],
      [V.flow(4, "Setting up a service area", "The steps on your Business Profile.",
              [("Choose type", "Service-area or hybrid business."), ("Address", "Remove it if customers do not visit."),
               ("Add areas", "Up to 20 cities, postal codes or areas."), ("Wait", "Approved edits can take up to 48 hours.")])],
      ["service area business Google", "GBP service area", "hide address Google Business Profile", "Google Maps service area"],
      "How do I set my service area on Google if I work from home in Anaheim?", "Google Business Profile service area"),

guide("google-business-profile-photos", "google business profile photos", "Google Business Profile photos", "Google Business Profile",
      "Google Business Profile Photos: Sizes and Tips",
      "Google Business Profile photos and videos for Anaheim and Orange County businesses: sizes, formats, logo, cover photo, review status and what to shoot.",
      "Google Business Profile Photos and Videos: Sizes, Rules and What to Upload",
      "Google Business Profile photos help customers recognize and choose your business. Google accepts JPG or PNG files between 10 KB and 5 MB, recommends 720 by 720 pixels, and wants photos that are in focus, well lit and not heavily filtered. Videos can run up to 30 seconds. Anaheim and Orange County businesses should add a logo, cover photo, exterior, interior, team and work photos, all real.",
      ["Photos show only after verification.", "Use JPG or PNG, 10 KB to 5 MB.", "720 by 720 pixels is recommended.",
       "Photos should represent reality.", "New uploads can take 24 to 48 hours to appear."],
      [("Why photos matter", "<p>Google's help page says photos and videos help complete your Business Profile and make it more attractive to customers, and that an exterior photo helps customers recognize your business when they visit. For a restaurant in Anaheim's Resort District or a contractor in Irvine, real photos are often what tips a searcher from comparing to calling.</p>"),
       ("Verify first", "<p>Google notes that your photos and videos show up on Google only after you verify your Business Profile. If uploads appear stuck, check your verification status before anything else.</p>"),
       ("Photo specifications", "<p>Google lists these standards: JPG or PNG format; between 10 KB and 5 MB; a recommended resolution of 720 pixels tall and 720 pixels wide; a minimum of 250 by 250 pixels. Photos should be in focus and well lit, with no significant alterations or excessive filters. In Google's words, the image should represent reality.</p>"),
       ("Video specifications", "<p>Videos can be up to 30 seconds long and up to 75 MB, at a resolution of 720p or higher. Short clips of your space, your team at work or a finished project work well.</p>"),
       ("Logo and cover photo", "<p>A logo helps customers recognize your business, and Google highlights it for businesses that have basic information such as a phone number and hours. A cover photo sits at the top of the profile, though Google notes that setting one does not guarantee it will show as the first image.</p>"),
       ("What to photograph", "<ul><li>The exterior from the street, so people recognize you on arrival.</li><li>The interior or workspace.</li><li>Your team, with permission.</li><li>Products, menu items or finished jobs.</li><li>Vehicles and equipment for service-area businesses.</li><li>Parking and entrances if they are hard to find.</li></ul><p>Update photos regularly; newest media appears first on your profile.</p>"),
       ("How to upload", "<p>On a computer, search for your business name and city in Google Search or Maps, then select Photos and Add photos. You can upload several at once. Businesses with 10 or more locations can bulk upload photos through a spreadsheet.</p>"),
       ("Upload status", "<p>Google reviews each upload. Pending means it is still processing or your profile is not verified; Not approved means it was flagged for breaking policies; Live means customers can see it. Google says it can take 24 to 48 hours for photos and videos to show.</p>"),
       ("Customer photos", "<p>Customers can add photos too. You cannot control all of them, but you can request removal of customer photos that break Google's policies. The best answer to poor customer photos is a steady flow of good ones of your own.</p>"),
       ("Photos, your website and AI", "<p>Use the same real photos on your website with descriptive file names and alt text. Consistent, real imagery across your profile and site helps people trust what they see, and gives AI assistants and Google's systems more context about your business.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Verify, then upload real, well-lit JPG or PNG photos around 720 pixels square, plus short videos, and keep adding new ones. Chimera, based in Anaheim, builds photo plans into Orange County profile work.</p>")],
      [("What size should Google Business Profile photos be?", "Google recommends 720 by 720 pixels, with a minimum of 250 by 250, as JPG or PNG between 10 KB and 5 MB."),
       ("Why aren't my photos showing?", "They show only after verification, and new uploads can take 24 to 48 hours."),
       ("Can I add videos to my profile?", "Yes, up to 30 seconds and 75 MB at 720p or higher."),
       ("Can I use edited or filtered photos?", "Google asks for photos without significant alterations or excessive filters."),
       ("Will my cover photo always show first?", "Not necessarily; Google says it is not guaranteed."),
       ("Can I remove customer photos?", "You can request removal of customer photos that break Google's policies.")],
      [GBP, POSTS, VERIFY, CHECK], ["g_photos", "g_verify"],
      [V.flow(7, "What happens after you upload", "The statuses Google shows for new photos and videos.",
              [("Upload", "JPG or PNG, 10 KB to 5 MB."), ("Pending", "Processing, or profile not yet verified."),
               ("Review", "Checked against Google's policies."), ("Live", "Visible on Search and Maps, usually within 24 to 48 hours.")])],
      ["Google Business Profile photo size", "GBP photos", "Google Maps business photos", "Google Business Profile videos"],
      "What size photos should I upload to my Google Business Profile?", "Google Business Profile photos"),

guide("google-business-profile-posts", "google business profile posts", "Google Business Profile posts", "Google Business Profile",
      "Google Business Profile Posts: Updates, Offers, Events",
      "How Anaheim and Orange County businesses can use Google Business Profile posts: updates, offers and events, scheduling, rules and ideas that drive calls.",
      "Google Business Profile Posts: How to Use Updates, Offers and Events",
      "Google Business Profile posts let you share updates, offers and events directly on Search and Maps. Updates can include a photo or video and a button, offers need a title and dates, and events need a title and start and end dates. Posts older than 6 months are archived unless they have a date range. Anaheim and Orange County businesses can schedule posts and repeat them weekly or monthly.",
      ["There are three post types.", "Offers and events need dates.", "Posts archive after 6 months without a date range.",
       "You can schedule and repeat posts.", "Unverified phone numbers can get posts rejected."],
      [("What posts are for", "<p>Google's help page says you can post updates on your Business Profile to share announcements, offers, updates and event details with customers on Search and Maps, and that seeing your latest updates helps customers decide to visit. For Orange County businesses, posts are a free way to show that the business is active, to promote seasonal offers and to answer common questions before customers call.</p>"),
       ("Where customers see posts", "<p>On mobile, posts appear in the Updates or Overview tabs of the profile on Search and Maps. On a computer, they appear in the From the owner section. Google also describes featured posts on mobile, pulled from your profile posts or social channels such as Facebook and Instagram, currently available in English for single-location food and drink businesses in the US, UK, Canada, Australia and New Zealand.</p>"),
       ("Updates", "<p>Updates share general information. You can add a description, a photo, a video and an action button with a link. Google says the button should take customers to a website where they can complete the action, such as booking, ordering online or learning more.</p>"),
       ("Offers", "<p>Offers promote sales or deals. Google requires a title, dates and time, and adds a View offer button automatically. You can also include a description, photo, video, coupon code, link and terms and conditions.</p>"),
       ("Events", "<p>Events promote something happening at your business. Add a title and start and end dates and times. Google notes that if you leave out start or end times, the event shows as lasting 24 hours on the event dates. You can add a description, photo, video and button.</p>"),
       ("How long posts last", "<p>Google says posts older than 6 months are archived unless a date range is set. Offers and events use their dates; updates without dates fall off after six months, so refresh evergreen information periodically.</p>"),
       ("Scheduling and repeating posts", "<p>When you create a post you can schedule it for a later date and time, and set it to repeat weekly, monthly or on a custom schedule. Multi-location businesses can share one post across several profiles. This makes it easy to plan a month of posts in one sitting.</p>"),
       ("Rules to follow", "<p>Google reviews posts against its content policy. It warns that posts with a phone number in the description might be rejected if the number cannot be verified as connected to the business. Keep posts accurate, avoid misleading claims and use real photos.</p>"),
       ("Post ideas for local businesses", "<ul><li>Seasonal services, such as AC tune-ups before summer.</li><li>Holiday hours and special menus.</li><li>New services or products.</li><li>Recent projects with a photo.</li><li>Events, classes or open houses.</li><li>Answers to common questions, linking to a page on your site.</li></ul>"),
       ("Posts and your website", "<p>Link each post to a relevant page on your site rather than the homepage, using a button. A post about a new service should lead to that service's page, which helps customers act and keeps your profile and website telling the same story.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Use updates for news, offers for deals and events for dates, schedule them ahead, link to the right page and refresh evergreen posts before they archive. Chimera, based in Anaheim, plans post calendars for Orange County businesses.</p>")],
      [("What are Google Business Profile posts?", "Updates, offers and events you publish on your profile to share news, deals and dates on Search and Maps."),
       ("How long do Google posts last?", "Google says posts older than 6 months are archived unless a date range is set."),
       ("Can I schedule Google Business Profile posts?", "Yes, and you can set them to repeat weekly, monthly or on a custom schedule."),
       ("Why was my post rejected?", "It may break Google's content policy; a phone number that cannot be verified as the business's can also cause rejection."),
       ("What does an offer post need?", "A title, dates and time; Google adds a View offer button."),
       ("Do posts help rankings?", "Google does not list posts as a ranking factor; they help customers decide and keep your profile current.")],
      [GBP, PHOTOS, HOURS, CHECK], ["g_posts", "g_gbp"],
      [V.flow(2, "The three types of post", "What each one needs, according to Google.",
              [("Update", "Description, photo or video and a button."), ("Offer", "Title, dates and time; View offer button added."),
               ("Event", "Title and start and end dates; no times means 24 hours."), ("Archive", "After 6 months unless a date range is set.")])],
      ["Google posts", "GBP posts", "Google Business Profile updates", "Google Business Profile offers"],
      "What should I post on my Google Business Profile?", "Google Business Profile posts"),

guide("google-business-profile-holiday-hours", "google business profile holiday hours", "Google Business Profile holiday hours", "Google Business Profile",
      "Google Business Profile Holiday Hours: Setup Guide",
      "Set Google Business Profile holiday hours and special hours for Anaheim and Orange County businesses: steps, the 6-day rule, temporary closures and tips.",
      "Google Business Profile Holiday Hours: How to Set Special Hours Correctly",
      "Google Business Profile holiday hours are set as special hours, which change your hours for specific dates without touching your regular schedule. Google says special hours suit closures of up to 6 days in a row; for 7 or more days, mark the business temporarily closed. Anaheim and Orange County businesses should confirm hours for every official holiday, even when they stay the same.",
      ["Use special hours for holidays and events.", "Special hours cover up to 6 days in a row.", "Confirm hours for every official holiday.",
       "Mark temporarily closed for 7 days or more.", "Use More hours for delivery or senior hours."],
      [("Why holiday hours matter", "<p>A customer who drives to a closed shop on a holiday rarely comes back, and one who sees open on Google when you are closed may leave a review about it. For Orange County businesses, the busy season around Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year, plus local events and conventions in Anaheim, makes accurate hours especially important.</p>"),
       ("What special hours are", "<p>Google's help page says special hours let you change your hours of operation for a brief period, like a special event, so your regular hours do not change. You can schedule them for holidays, special events and other exceptional circumstances.</p>"),
       ("The 6-day rule", "<p>Google says you can set special hours if your business temporarily adjusts its hours or remains closed for up to 6 days in a row. If you will be closed for 7 or more days, or for an unknown period, mark the business as temporarily closed instead.</p>"),
       ("Confirm hours even when nothing changes", "<p>Google recommends confirming your hours for official holidays even if they are the same as your regular hours, because it makes clear to customers that your hours are accurate. Without confirmation, Google may show a note that hours could differ.</p>"),
       ("How to set special hours", "<p>Go to your Business Profile, select Edit profile, then Hours. Next to Special hours, select Edit. Review a suggested date or add a custom date, then set your hours: uncheck Closed and enter times if you are open, or leave Closed checked if you are not. You can add more than one set of hours for a day. Save, and the special hours appear in your business information.</p>"),
       ("Open with main hours", "<p>Google notes that for special hours to show, your business hours must be set to Open with main hours. If your profile has no regular hours, add them first.</p>"),
       ("Hours that run past midnight", "<p>A set of special hours cannot exceed 24 hours. If you are open from the evening into the next day, Google says to split the hours into two sets: one that runs to the end of the first day and one that starts at midnight on the second. Regular hours for the second day are then ignored in favor of the special hours.</p>"),
       ("More hours for specific services", "<p>If parts of your business keep different times, such as senior hours, delivery or takeout, Google suggests setting More hours instead of special hours. Restaurants and stores often need both.</p>"),
       ("Multi-location businesses", "<p>Businesses with many locations can set special hours in a bulk upload spreadsheet using the Special hours column, with dates and times in Google's format and an x for all-day closures. Google warns that empty columns in a bulk upload can erase existing information, so include only what you mean to change.</p>"),
       ("Match your website and other listings", "<p>Update holiday hours on your website, Apple Maps, Yelp and other listings at the same time. AI assistants and search engines compare sources, and conflicting hours make all of them less reliable.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Set special hours for each holiday, confirm unchanged holidays, use temporarily closed for 7 days or more and update every other listing to match. Chimera, based in Anaheim, keeps holiday hours current for Orange County clients.</p>")],
      [("How do I add holiday hours on Google?", "Edit profile, Hours, then Special hours; choose the date and set your hours or mark it closed."),
       ("How long can special hours last?", "Google says special hours suit changes or closures of up to 6 days in a row."),
       ("What if I'm closed for two weeks?", "Mark the business as temporarily closed; Google recommends this for 7 days or more."),
       ("Should I set holiday hours if they don't change?", "Yes. Google recommends confirming hours for official holidays even if they are the same."),
       ("Why aren't my special hours showing?", "Your business hours must be set to Open with main hours."),
       ("What are More hours?", "Separate hours for services such as delivery, takeout or senior hours.")],
      [GBP, POSTS, NAP, CHECK], ["g_special", "g_gbp"],
      [V.timeline(2, "Choosing the right setting", "Which Google setting to use for each kind of change.",
                  [("1 day", "Special hours", "A holiday or event closure or change."), ("2 to 6 days", "Special hours", "Set each date individually."),
                   ("7+ days", "Temporarily closed", "Or an unknown period."), ("Every holiday", "Confirm hours", "Even when they stay the same.")])],
      ["Google special hours", "holiday hours Google Maps", "GBP holiday hours", "temporarily closed Google Business Profile"],
      "How do I change my holiday hours on Google Maps?", "Google Business Profile holiday hours"),

guide("how-to-remove-fake-google-reviews", "how to remove fake google reviews", "remove fake Google reviews", "Reviews",
      "How to Remove Fake Google Reviews (Step by Step)",
      "How Anaheim and Orange County businesses can report and remove fake Google reviews: what qualifies, the Reviews Management Tool, appeals and extortion scams.",
      "How to Remove Fake Google Reviews: Report, Track and Appeal",
      "To remove fake Google reviews, report each one from your Business Profile or Google's Reviews Management Tool and choose the reason, such as spam. Google removes only reviews that break its policies, not reviews you simply disagree with. Evaluation usually takes several days, and if a report is rejected you can submit a one-time appeal. Anaheim and Orange County businesses should also reply calmly in public.",
      ["Only policy-breaking reviews are removed.", "Report from your profile or the Reviews Management Tool.",
       "Evaluation usually takes several days.", "You get one appeal per review.", "Report extortion attempts separately."],
      [("What Google will and won't remove", "<p>Google's help page says you can report any review, but only those that violate Google policies are eligible for removal. It also says not to report a review just because you disagree with it, and that Google does not get involved in conflicts between businesses and customers. Its Maps content policy bans fake engagement, such as reviews that do not reflect a genuine experience, so fake reviews are a legitimate reason to report.</p>"),
       ("Signs a review may be fake", "<ul><li>The reviewer has no record of being a customer.</li><li>Details do not match your business, services or location.</li><li>Several similar reviews arrive in a short time.</li><li>The review comes from a competitor or someone with a conflict of interest.</li><li>The text is off-topic, abusive or spam.</li></ul><p>Note what you find for each review; it helps when choosing a reason and appealing.</p>"),
       ("How to report from your profile", "<p>Go to your Business Profile, select Read reviews, then next to the review select Report. Choose the reason, such as Spam or Profanity, and send the report. Google says evaluation typically takes several days.</p>"),
       ("Using the Reviews Management Tool", "<p>Google's Reviews Management Tool lets you report reviews and track their status. Sign in with the account that manages your profile, select your business and choose to report a new review for removal. Statuses include Decision pending, Report reviewed with no policy violation, and Escalated, which means you should check your email for the final decision.</p>"),
       ("Appealing a decision", "<p>If a flagged review is found not to break policy and you disagree, Google allows a one-time appeal through the same tool. You can select up to 10 reviews in an appeal. Google emails the result: reviews that violate policy are removed, and those that comply stay live.</p>"),
       ("Extortion and review attacks", "<p>Google's help page has a separate route for businesses targeted by extortion scams involving negative reviews, where someone threatens bad reviews unless paid. Do not pay. Report the incident through Google's process and keep records of the messages.</p>"),
       ("Reporting a reviewer's profile", "<p>In the Google Maps app, you can report a user profile that contributes inappropriate content: open the Business tab, then Reviews, tap the reviewer's name and use the menu to report them, choosing a reason such as a legal issue or privacy concern.</p>"),
       ("Reply while you wait", "<p>Removal takes time and is not guaranteed. A short, calm public reply, saying you have no record of the customer and inviting them to contact you, shows other readers that you take feedback seriously. Never reveal private details about real customers.</p>"),
       ("Never fight fake reviews with fake reviews", "<p>Google's policies prohibit incentivized and fake reviews in both directions. Buying positive reviews to bury negative ones can lead to removed reviews or restrictions on your profile. The lasting answer is a steady flow of genuine reviews from real customers.</p>"),
       ("Build a review habit", "<p>Ask every real customer for a review with a direct link or QR code, reply to reviews and keep notes on unusual activity. A business in Anaheim or anywhere in Orange County with many genuine recent reviews is far less affected by a single fake one.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Report fake reviews with the right reason, track them in the Reviews Management Tool, appeal once if needed, reply calmly and keep earning real reviews. Chimera, based in Anaheim, helps Orange County businesses manage reviews within Google's rules.</p>")],
      [("Can I remove a bad Google review?", "Only if it breaks Google's policies; Google does not remove reviews because a business disagrees with them."),
       ("How do I report a fake Google review?", "From your Business Profile or the Reviews Management Tool, select Report and choose the reason."),
       ("How long does Google take to review a report?", "Google says evaluation typically takes several days."),
       ("What if Google doesn't remove it?", "You can submit a one-time appeal through the Reviews Management Tool."),
       ("What should I do about review extortion?", "Do not pay; report it through Google's process for extortion scams."),
       ("Should I reply to a fake review?", "Yes, briefly and calmly, without sharing private customer details.")],
      [REPLY, REVIEWS, GBP, L("how to fix wrong information about your brand in ChatGPT", "how-to-fix-wrong-information-about-your-brand-in-chatgpt")],
      ["g_report", "g_fake", "g_rank"],
      [V.flow(3, "From report to decision", "How Google handles a reported review.",
              [("Report", "From your profile or the Reviews Management Tool."), ("Evaluate", "Usually several days."),
               ("Decision", "Removed if it breaks policy."), ("Appeal", "One-time appeal, up to 10 reviews.")])],
      ["report fake Google reviews", "delete Google review", "remove negative Google review", "Google review removal"],
      "How do I get a fake review removed from my Google Business Profile?", "Remove fake Google reviews"),

guide("google-local-services-ads-vs-seo", "local services ads vs seo", "Local Services Ads vs SEO", "Local search",
      "Local Services Ads vs SEO: Which Is Better?",
      "Google Local Services Ads vs SEO for Anaheim and Orange County businesses: how each works, costs, the Google Verified badge, eligibility and using both.",
      "Google Local Services Ads vs SEO: How to Choose, and When to Use Both",
      "Local Services Ads vs SEO comes down to speed and control. Google Local Services Ads put you in a paid unit at the top of search results and charge per lead, but only certain categories qualify. SEO earns free visibility in Maps and organic results over time, and Google says you cannot pay for a better local ranking. Most Anaheim and Orange County service businesses do best with both.",
      ["Local Services Ads charge per lead.", "Only certain categories are eligible.", "Google Verified badges build trust.",
       "Organic local ranking cannot be bought.", "Both draw on reviews and responsiveness."],
      [("What Local Services Ads are", "<p>Google's Local Services help page says the ads let you advertise on Google and receive leads directly from potential customers, as phone calls and messages. The Local Services unit is shown prominently in search results when people search for services you offer in the areas you choose. Customers can call, message or, in many categories, book directly through your ad.</p>"),
       ("How you pay", "<p>Google says advertisers pay only for leads related to their business and the services they offer, rather than for clicks. Customers choose your profile from the others shown, so you hear only from people who selected you.</p>"),
       ("Who is eligible", "<p>Google says Local Services Ads are available only for certain service categories, and you check eligibility for your area. The US list includes trades such as electricians, plumbers, roofers, carpet cleaners and water damage services, and professional services such as estate, criminal and immigration lawyers. Some categories are limited, such as architects and tattoo studios in California and Florida only.</p>"),
       ("The Google Verified badge", "<p>Google describes the Google Verified badge as a signal that a business has passed Google's screening process, shown with the specific checks completed. Google notes the badge is currently unavailable for auto, beauty and dining categories. Pre-badge ads let some businesses receive leads after preliminary checks, but those listings appear below providers who have completed all requirements and earned the badge.</p>"),
       ("Responsiveness affects ad ranking", "<p>Google advises responding to as many messages as you can, even when you decline a job, and warns that regularly failing to answer calls or respond to messages may affect your ad ranking. Ads reward businesses that pick up the phone.</p>"),
       ("How SEO is different", "<p>Organic local SEO covers your Business Profile, website and reputation. Google says local results are mainly based on relevance, distance and popularity, and that there is no way to request or pay for a better local ranking. SEO takes longer, but the visibility it earns does not stop when a budget runs out, and it feeds the sources AI assistants use.</p>"),
       ("Comparing the two", "<table><tr><th></th><th>Local Services Ads</th><th>Local SEO</th></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Per lead</td><td>Time and work, no per-lead fee</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>Fast once approved</td><td>Builds over months</td></tr><tr><td>Eligibility</td><td>Certain categories only</td><td>Any business</td></tr><tr><td>Placement</td><td>Paid unit at the top</td><td>Maps, local pack and organic results</td></tr><tr><td>When budget stops</td><td>Leads stop</td><td>Visibility continues</td></tr></table>"),
       ("When ads make sense", "<p>Ads suit businesses in eligible categories that need calls quickly, can answer every lead and have the reviews and licenses to pass screening. A new HVAC company in Anaheim or a plumber entering a new Orange County city can fill the calendar while SEO builds.</p>"),
       ("When SEO matters most", "<p>SEO matters most for businesses outside the eligible categories, for those that want to reduce lead costs over time, and for anyone who wants to be recommended by AI assistants, which draw on websites, listings and reviews rather than ads.</p>"),
       ("Using both together", "<p>Reviews, accurate business details and fast responses help both. Keep your Business Profile, website and ad profile consistent, track leads from each source separately and shift budget as organic calls grow.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Local Services Ads buy fast, per-lead visibility in eligible categories; SEO earns lasting visibility that cannot be bought. Chimera, based in Anaheim, focuses on the organic and AI side for Orange County businesses.</p>")],
      [("What is the difference between Local Services Ads and SEO?", "Local Services Ads are a paid, per-lead placement for eligible categories; SEO earns unpaid visibility in Maps and organic results."),
       ("How much do Local Services Ads cost?", "You pay per lead related to your services, within the budget you set."),
       ("Can I pay Google to rank higher on Maps?", "No. Google says there is no way to request or pay for a better local ranking."),
       ("Is my business eligible for Local Services Ads?", "Only certain categories are eligible; check eligibility for your area on Google's Local Services site."),
       ("What is the Google Verified badge?", "A badge showing a business passed Google's screening; it is not available for auto, beauty and dining categories."),
       ("Should I do both?", "Many service businesses use ads for speed and SEO for lasting, lower-cost visibility.")],
      [LOCALSEO, GBP, L("GEO vs PPC", "geo-vs-ppc"), L("is SEO worth it for small business", "is-seo-worth-it-for-small-business")],
      ["g_lsa", "g_rank"],
      [local_rank_flow(5)],
      ["Google Local Services Ads", "LSA vs SEO", "Google Guaranteed vs SEO", "Local Services Ads Orange County"],
      "Should my plumbing business use Google Local Services Ads or SEO?", "Local Services Ads vs SEO"),

guide("core-web-vitals-for-local-businesses", "core web vitals", "Core Web Vitals", "Technical SEO",
      "Core Web Vitals for Local Businesses: A Guide",
      "Core Web Vitals explained for Anaheim and Orange County business websites: LCP, INP and CLS targets, how to measure them and fixes that help.",
      "Core Web Vitals for Local Business Websites: Targets, Tests and Fixes",
      "Core Web Vitals are Google's measures of real-world page experience: Largest Contentful Paint for loading, Interaction to Next Paint for responsiveness and Cumulative Layout Shift for visual stability. Google says to aim for LCP within 2.5 seconds, INP under 200 milliseconds and CLS under 0.1. For Anaheim and Orange County businesses, most fixes come down to lighter images, fewer scripts and stable layouts.",
      ["Three metrics: LCP, INP and CLS.", "Targets: 2.5 seconds, 200 milliseconds and 0.1.",
       "Google highly recommends good scores.", "Search Console shows your real data.", "Most local sites fix images and scripts first."],
      [("What Core Web Vitals are", "<p>Google's Search Central documentation describes Core Web Vitals as a set of metrics that measure real-world user experience for loading performance, interactivity and visual stability. Google highly recommends that site owners achieve good Core Web Vitals for success with Search and for a good user experience, and says this aligns with what its core ranking systems seek to reward.</p>"),
       ("Largest Contentful Paint", "<p>LCP measures loading performance: how long it takes for the main content, often a hero image or headline, to appear. Google says to strive for LCP within the first 2.5 seconds of the page starting to load. On local business sites, an oversized hero photo is the most common reason for a slow LCP.</p>"),
       ("Interaction to Next Paint", "<p>INP measures responsiveness: how quickly the page reacts when someone taps a button or opens a menu. Google's target is an INP of less than 200 milliseconds. Heavy chat widgets, tracking scripts and page builders often slow it down.</p>"),
       ("Cumulative Layout Shift", "<p>CLS measures visual stability: how much the page jumps around while loading. Google's target is a CLS score of less than 0.1. Images without set dimensions, late-loading banners and pop-ups are typical causes, and they lead to mis-taps on phone numbers and buttons.</p>"),
       ("Why it matters for local businesses", "<p>Most local searches happen on phones, often on the go. A customer in Anaheim looking for a locksmith or a restaurant will not wait for a slow page or fight a shifting layout. Good Core Web Vitals help people reach your phone number, booking form or menu without friction.</p>"),
       ("How to measure", "<p>Google points site owners to the Core Web Vitals report in Search Console, which shows how your pages perform for real visitors, and lists tools such as PageSpeed Insights for testing individual pages. Check mobile results first, because that is where most local customers are.</p>"),
       ("Fixes for LCP", "<ul><li>Resize and compress hero images; serve modern formats.</li><li>Do not lazy-load the main image at the top of the page.</li><li>Use good hosting and caching.</li><li>Remove unused fonts and plugins.</li></ul>"),
       ("Fixes for INP", "<ul><li>Remove scripts you do not use, such as old tracking tags.</li><li>Delay chat widgets and pop-ups until after the page loads.</li><li>Keep page builders lean and avoid heavy sliders.</li></ul>"),
       ("Fixes for CLS", "<ul><li>Set width and height on images and embeds.</li><li>Reserve space for banners and cookie notices.</li><li>Avoid inserting content above what people are reading.</li></ul>"),
       ("Speed and AI search", "<p>AI crawlers and search engines fetch your pages to read them. A lean page with real HTML text and links loads quickly and is easier to parse. Google's link guidance notes that it can generally only crawl links that are standard anchor elements with an href, another reason to keep pages simple.</p>"),
       ("The short version", "<p>Aim for LCP within 2.5 seconds, INP under 200 milliseconds and CLS under 0.1, measure in Search Console, and fix images, scripts and layout shifts first. Chimera, based in Anaheim, checks page experience in every Orange County site audit.</p>")],
      [("What are the Core Web Vitals?", "Largest Contentful Paint, Interaction to Next Paint and Cumulative Layout Shift."),
       ("What is a good LCP?", "Google says to aim for LCP within 2.5 seconds."),
       ("What is a good INP?", "Under 200 milliseconds."),
       ("What is a good CLS?", "Under 0.1."),
       ("Do Core Web Vitals affect rankings?", "Google highly recommends good scores and says they align with what its core ranking systems seek to reward."),
       ("Where can I see my Core Web Vitals?", "In the Core Web Vitals report in Google Search Console, and in tools such as PageSpeed Insights.")],
      [CHECK, L("AI search optimization checklist", "ai-search-optimization-checklist"), L("internal linking for AI search", "internal-linking-for-ai-search"), LOCALSEO],
      ["g_cwv", "g_links"],
      [V.flow(1, "The three Core Web Vitals", "Google's targets for a good experience.",
              [("LCP", "Loading: within 2.5 seconds."), ("INP", "Responsiveness: under 200 milliseconds."),
               ("CLS", "Visual stability: under 0.1."), ("Measure", "Search Console's Core Web Vitals report.")])],
      ["Core Web Vitals local SEO", "page speed for local business", "LCP INP CLS", "website speed Orange County"],
      "Why is my business website slow on phones, and does it hurt my Google ranking?", "Core Web Vitals"),
]

